 "answer": "They may be using **different scales, pollutants, or time windows**. In the U.S., the EPA’s AQI calls **51–100 “Moderate,”** while **101–150 is “Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups”** and **151–200 is “Unhealthy.”** Apps may also show an “unhealthy” warning for a particular pollutant—often PM2.5—or for people with asthma or other sensitivities, even when a separate overall reading says “Moderate.” ([airnow.gov](https://www.airnow.gov/aqi/aqi-basics//?utm_source=openai))\n\nAlso, one reading might be **live or based on nearby sensors**, while another is an average or forecast. EPA’s current particle-pollution reading uses recent hourly data, so readings can differ as conditions change. ([airnow.gov](https://www.airnow.gov/aqi/aqi-basics/using-air-quality-index/?utm_source=openai))\n\nCheck whether both screens show the same **location, timestamp, pollutant (especially PM2.5), and index scale**. If you share the app names or a screenshot, I can help pinpoint the mismatch.",
